WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Speaker 1: Lindsey Graham has been in a bit of&nbsp;
trouble recently, but he actually got a minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:09.040
victory yesterday when an appeals court ruled that&nbsp;
the South Carolina senator can have a chance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.480
challenge the order that he got to testify in the&nbsp;
Georgia election fraud case because of protections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.040
for lawmakers under the Constitution, speech&nbsp;
or debate clause. Graham has been ordered to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.000
based on a subpoena that he got appear tomorrow to&nbsp;
talk about the calls he made to Georgia election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.200
officials in the wake of the 2020 election.&nbsp;
And this is all part of an investigation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.360
seemingly not so much into Graham, but into&nbsp;
the person who was in charge of the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.560
scheme. And that is, of course, Donald Trump.&nbsp;
And first, Graham and his lawyers were saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.120
that what he did with his phone calls to people&nbsp;
like Secretary of State Brad Raffensperger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.400
they were simply part of his job duties&nbsp;
as a U.S. senator because as a lawmaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.360
it's important that he is able to make sure that&nbsp;
the election systems in all the different states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.520
are on the up and up. But then that argument&nbsp;
was quickly turned down because, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.960
Graham represents South Carolina, not Georgia. So&nbsp;
why is the election process in Georgia any of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.640
business? It's not. And that's why that argument&nbsp;
was turned down. But fast forward and Graham filed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.480
an emergency request with the U.S. Court of&nbsp;
Appeals for the 11th Circuit, asking it to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:18.480
the subpoena on hold while his appeal played out.&nbsp;
And now the appeals court has ruled it wants both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.760
sides to argue what areas of questioning would&nbsp;
be appropriate for a U.S. senator, a sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.200
U.S. senator, especially. And so there's going to&nbsp;
be a stay. This was supposed to happen tomorrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.480
and it's not going to happen as was initially&nbsp;
planned. Graham's attorneys say that he will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.920
suffer irreparable harm if forced to appear&nbsp;
before his appeal concludes, which may be true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.320
but I think it's going to be irreparable&nbsp;
harm in the form of Trump and Trump voters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.360
including the voters of South Carolina, which is&nbsp;
quite a red state, and they may not be so happy if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.880
Graham decides to spill the beans on Trump in this&nbsp;
testimony. And the district attorney's office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:01.200
as you might imagine, is getting sick of Graham's&nbsp;
delay tactics. They've been going on for weeks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.280
weeks now. The office is saying that Graham&nbsp;
is crucial to the investigation and that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.400
is expected to provide information regarding&nbsp;
additional sources of relevant information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:17.040
It is true that if he has to eventually testify,&nbsp;
then he may just decide to be obstinate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.080
like he may just plead the fifth a bunch of times&nbsp;
as we recently saw Donald Trump do and a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.760
investigation. But if he decides to ignore&nbsp;
this thing altogether after being forced into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.680
delivering a testimony, maybe he'll open&nbsp;
himself up to criminal charges. So I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.040
he will at least cooperate in some way if it's&nbsp;
determined that he has to, because Graham loves to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:42.800
just grovel to whoever has influence and authority&nbsp;
over him at any given moment. Like for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.600
that person seemed to be John McCain. The two&nbsp;
were inseparable. Right. And then, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.000
became. Passed away. So that put an end to that&nbsp;
relationship. And then Graham, when he ran for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.480
president in 2016. Trump dox them so you'd think&nbsp;
that that would put Graham on opposing sides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.800
to Trump like you wouldn't be a Trump fan after&nbsp;
that. But then eventually Graham came around to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.200
liking Trump being one of his lackeys because&nbsp;
Trump became the figurehead of the Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.400
Party. So what else could he do? He had to grovel&nbsp;
at his feet. And then after January 6th, it looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.040
like the party and especially the politicians in&nbsp;
power were moving away from Donald Trump. So what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.520
did Lindsey Graham do? He made this big speech on&nbsp;
the night of January six talking about how this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.880
was unacceptable and him and Trump had had this&nbsp;
long road, but that was over with. And Joe Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.520
and Kamala Harris legitimately won the 2020&nbsp;
election. And then it only took a few weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.880
maybe a couple of months before he decided to go&nbsp;
back to groveling at Donald Trump's feet, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:41.840
he knew that the party and the constituents&nbsp;
were not ready to move away from Trump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:47.360
And so that's Lindsey Graham for you. If he just&nbsp;
realizes that, hey, these prosecutors in Georgia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.520
they have the goods on me and they have&nbsp;
authority over me right now. He's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.560
decide to change his ways. Ultimately, though,&nbsp;
if they're not able to get Graham to testify,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.720
I don't think that this is going to change the&nbsp;
course of the investigation. If they want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.320
charge Donald Trump in this, they seem to have&nbsp;
a rock solid case. I mean, they have that phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:10.160
call with Raffensperger where Trump asked for&nbsp;
the 11,280 votes and all these other calls and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.080
repeated attempts, like if they want to get Trump&nbsp;
on this, it seems like they can, but you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.320
as well try to make it an even more rock solid&nbsp;
case. And that's why they're trying to get Graham&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.520
on board with this, too. But at least for now,&nbsp;
Graham's able to fend off the order to testify.

